Decision: keep `--mark P` on `deadline wait`, or remove it?

Facts given:
- Vision/use case: an agent cannot feel time pass, so the guard tells it: a one-line clock after every Bash or file-write call, and a time-up line after the deadline. The owner chose this over reminders at 50%, 80% and 100%, which "is not able to well predict, specific when exploring solutions" (2026-10-17); the reminders are removed.
- What is left: `bun .claude/skills/workflow-design/guard/src/cli.ts deadline wait [--mark P]` — a command the agent runs in the background as a timer; it sleeps until the deadline, or until P percent of it with `--mark P`, then prints the status line. It is not a reminder: nothing fires unless the agent starts the wait. The code is `.claude/skills/workflow-design/guard/src/cli.ts` (the `wait` branch of the deadline command).
- The two readings: (A) `--mark P` is a leftover of the removed marks and should go, leaving `deadline wait` to fire at the deadline only; (B) it is a separate timer the agent chooses for itself (for example to wake at 90% and write the report), and should stay.
- Use in this project so far: the time-monitor run used a plain background `sleep` as its timer, not `deadline wait`; no run has used `--mark` (measured: `grep -rn "deadline wait --mark" runs/` finds none).

