Now the substance: keep or remove `--mark P`?

Option B (keep) seems right to me. Reasons:
1. The owner's objection was to *automatic* fixed marks ("is not able to well predict, specific when exploring solutions") — the reminders fire regardless of the agent's state. `--mark` is not automatic: nothing fires unless the agent starts the wait. It's a self-chosen timer; the prediction is made by the agent that knows its work, not by the guard's fixed schedule. So the defect the owner named does not exist in `--mark`.
2. Removing `--mark` would leave `deadline wait` useful only at 100%, which duplicates what the clock line already tells the agent ("time is up" after every call). The wait's unique value is the ability to sleep to a *chosen* moment and get control back while the deadline still has time left — e.g., wake at 90% to write the report. Without `--mark`, the timer only returns at the deadline, when the time-up line arrives anyway via the hook on the next call. So removing `--mark` makes `wait` largely redundant with T5's clock.
   - Careful: a background wait at 100% would still be useful if the agent is doing a long single operation with no tool calls (a long command), where the after-call clock line can't fire. In that window, a background timer is the only signal. So `wait` without `--mark` isn't fully redundant: it wakes *when the time is up* even if no call occurs. But the mark extends the same capacity to a point before the deadline, which is exactly when a report needs writing. So keep.
3. Cost of keeping: one flag, 10 lines of code, one usage string, documented in SPEC T3. No measured runs use it. The cost is maintenance surface and a second way to do the same thing (plain `sleep` also works). But the cost is tiny and already paid.
4. Cost of removing: it removes the only way, inside the guard, to wake before the deadline at a chosen fraction. It would force agents back to plain `sleep` arithmetic (which the time-monitor run already did), losing the deadline-aware computation and the status line. It also breaks SPEC T3 and any user who learns the flag; and it is a one-way deletion that can be re-added but at the cost of a new decision.
5. The measurement cuts both ways: no run used `--mark`, but no run used `deadline wait` at all (the time-monitor run used plain `sleep`). So the zero is not evidence that `--mark` is unwanted; it is evidence that the whole wait path has not been adopted. Removing `--mark` on the strength of that zero would treat an unmeasured feature's non-use as a verdict, which the evidence does not support. If anything, the fact that an agent chose plain `sleep` over `deadline wait` suggests the problem is *discoverability/usability* of the wait path, not the `--mark` flag. That's a key insight and a proposed change to the pattern maybe.

